and not in the Soviet Union is that in China, often the reforms are from the bottom up, as it were. They're not just being driven by people sitting around a kind of committee table in the capital city dictating to the provinces or surprising local elites with reforms that may actually dissipate the local elite's power. The local elites don't like them. Is that right? Are some of these reforms being driven out by what's going on out in the countryside and individual localities or provinces or towns or whatever? Yes, absolutely. One of the liveliest debates at the moment in this particular

